window.docContents[240].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___6___-Algebras-defined-by-generators-and-relations/Section-2___6___-Algebras-defined-by-generators-and-relations/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___6":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.6. Algebras defined by generators and relations\u0009Section 2.6: Algebras defined by generators and relations","header":"Section 2.6: Algebras defined by generators and relations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___6___-Algebras-defined-by-generators-and-relations/Section-2___6___-Algebras-defined-by-generators-and-relations/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___6"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___12___-Problems/Representations-of-the-Heisenberg-group/#Chapter4___Problem4___12___2":{"contents":"Problem 4.12.2. Let p be a prime. Let G be the group of 3 \\times 3 matrices over \\mathbb{F}_p which are upper triangular and have 1's on the diagonal, under multiplication (its order is p^3). It is called the Heisenberg group. For any complex number z such that z^p = 1, we define a representation of G on the space V of complex functions on \\mathbb{F}_p by\n\n(\\rho \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} f)(x) = f(x - 1),\n(\\rho \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} f)(x) = z^x f(x)\n\n(note that z^x makes sense since z^p = 1).\n\n(a) Show that such a representation exists and is unique, and compute \\rho(g) for all g \\in G.\n\n(b) Denote this representation by R_z. Show that R_z is irreducible if and only if z \\neq 1.\n\n(c) Classify all 1-dimensional representations of G. Show that R_1 decomposes into a direct sum of 1-dimensional representations, where each of them occurs exactly once.\n\n(d) Use (a)—(c) and the \"sum of squares\" formula to classify all irreducible representations of G.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.12. Problems\u0009Representations of the Heisenberg group","header":"Representations of the Heisenberg group","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___12___-Problems/Representations-of-the-Heisenberg-group/#Chapter4___Problem4___12___2"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___8___-Character-tables___-examples/Character-tables-of-Q_8___-S_4___-and-A_5/#Chapter4___Example4___8___1":{"contents":"Example 4.8.1. The following three character tables are of Q_8, S_4, and A_5, respectively:\n\n* Q_8* 1* -1* i* j* k\n* #* 1* 1* 2* 2* 2\n* \\mathbb{C}_{++}* 1* 1* 1* 1* 1\n* \\mathbb{C}_{+-}* 1* 1* 1* -1* -1\n* \\mathbb{C}_{-+}* 1* 1* -1* 1* -1\n* \\mathbb{C}_{--}* 1* 1* -1* -1* 1\n* \\mathbb{C}^2* 2* -2* 0* 0* 0\n\n* S_4* Id* (12)* (12)(34)* (123)* (1234)\n* #* 1* 6* 3* 8* 6\n* \\mathbb{C}_+* 1* 1* 1* 1* 1\n* \\mathbb{C}_-* 1* -1* 1* 1* -1\n* \\mathbb{C}^2* 2* 0* 2* -1* 0\n* \\mathbb{C}^3_+* 3* -1* -1* 0* 1\n* \\mathbb{C}^3_-* 3* 1* -1* 0* -1\n\n* A_5* Id* (123)* (12)(34)* (12345)* (13245)\n* #* 1* 20* 15* 12* 12\n* \\mathbb{C}* 1* 1* 1* 1* 1\n* \\mathbb{C}^3_+* 3* 0* -1* \\frac{1+\\sqrt{5}}{2}* \\frac{1-\\sqrt{5}}{2}\n* \\mathbb{C}^3_-* 3* 0* -1* \\frac{1-\\sqrt{5}}{2}* \\frac{1+\\sqrt{5}}{2}\n* \\mathbb{C}^4* 4* 1* 0* -1* -1\n* \\mathbb{C}^5* 5* -1* 1* 0* 0\n\nIndeed, the computation of the characters of the 1-dimensional representations is straightforward.\n\nThe character of the 2-dimensional representation of Q_8 is obtained from the explicit formula (4.3.1) for this representation, or by using orthogonality.\n\nFor S_4, the 2-dimensional irreducible representation is obtained from the 2-dimensional irreducible representation of S_3 via the surjective homomorphism S_4 \\to S_3, which allows one to obtain its character from the character table of S_3.\n\nThe character of the 3-dimensional representation \\mathbb{C}^3_+ is computed from its geometric realization by rotations of the cube. Namely, by rotating the cube, S_4 permutes the main diagonals. Thus (12) is\nthe rotation by 180^0 around an axis that is perpendicular to two opposite edges, (12)(34) is the rotation by 180^0 around an axis that is perpendicular to two opposite faces, (123) is the rotation around a main diagonal by 120^0, and (1234) is the rotation by 90^0 around an axis that is perpendicular to two opposite faces; this allows us to compute the traces easily, using the fact that the trace of a rotation by the angle \\phi in \\mathbb{R}^3 is 1 + 2\\cos\\phi. Now the character of \\mathbb{C}^3_- is found by multiplying the character of \\mathbb{C}^3_+ by the character of the sign representation.\n\nFinally, we explain how to obtain the character table of A_5 (even permutations of five items). The group A_5 is the group of rotations of the regular icosahedron. Thus it has a 3-dimensional \"rotation representation\" \\mathbb{C}^3_+, in which (12)(34) is the rotation by 180^0 around an axis perpendicular to two opposite edges, (123) is the rotation by 120^0 around an axis perpendicular to two opposite faces, and (12345), (13254) are the rotations by 72^0, respectively 144^0, around axes going through two opposite vertices. The character of this representation is computed from this description in a straightforward way.\n\nAnother representation of A_5, which is also 3-dimensional, is \\mathbb{C}^3_+ twisted by the automorphism of A_5 given by conjugation by (12) inside S_5. This representation is denoted by \\mathbb{C}^3_-. It has the same character as \\mathbb{C}^3_+, except that the conjugacy classes (12345) and (13245) are interchanged.\n\nThere are two remaining irreducible representations, and by the sum of squares formula their dimensions are 4 and 5. So we call them \\mathbb{C}^4 and \\mathbb{C}^5.\n\nThe representation \\mathbb{C}^4 is realized on the space of functions on the set \\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5\\} with zero sum of values, where A_5 acts by permutations (check that it is irreducible!). The character of this representation is equal to the character of the 5-dimensional permutation representation minus the character of the 1-dimensional trivial representation (constant functions). The former at an element g is equal to the number of items among 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 which are fixed by g.\n\nThe representation \\mathbb{C}^5 is realized on the space of functions on pairs of opposite vertices of the icosahedron which has zero sum of\nvalues (check that it is irreducible!). The character of this representation is computed similarly to the character of \\mathbb{C}^4, or from the orthogonality formula.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.8. Character tables, examples\u0009Character tables of Q_8, S_4, and A_5","header":"Character tables of Q_8, S_4, and A_5","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___8___-Character-tables___-examples/Character-tables-of-Q_8___-S_4___-and-A_5/#Chapter4___Example4___8___1"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Character-vanishes-or-element-acts-as-scalar-when-gcd_LPAR____C______-dim-V_RPAR_-___-1/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem--Character-vanishes-or-element-acts-as-scalar-when-gcd_LPAR____C______-dim-V_RPAR_-___-1--Character-vanishes-or-element-acts-as-scalar-when-gcd_LPAR____C______-dim-V_RPAR_-___-1--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"For a simple finite-group representation, coprimality of the conjugacy-class size and dimension forces a zero character value or scalar action.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_before_Lemma5.4.5; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5.4.4; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5.4.6; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Theorem5.4.4; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.4. Burnside's theorem\u0009Character vanishes or element acts as scalar when gcd(|C|, dim V) = 1\u0009Character vanishes or element acts as scalar when gcd(|C|, dim V) = 1\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Character-vanishes-or-element-acts-as-scalar-when-gcd_LPAR____C______-dim-V_RPAR_-___-1/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem--Character-vanishes-or-element-acts-as-scalar-when-gcd_LPAR____C______-dim-V_RPAR_-___-1--Character-vanishes-or-element-acts-as-scalar-when-gcd_LPAR____C______-dim-V_RPAR_-___-1--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___4___-Roots/Definition-6___4___10___-Reflection-and-simple-reflections/#Chapter6___Definition6___4___10":{"contents":"Definition 6.4.10. Let \\alpha \\in \\mathbb{Z}^n be a positive root. The reflection s_\\alpha is defined by the formula\n\ns_\\alpha(v) = v - B(v, \\alpha)\\alpha.\n\nWe denote s_{\\alpha_i} by s_i and call these simple reflections.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.4. Roots\u0009Definition 6.4.10: Reflection and simple reflections","header":"Definition 6.4.10: Reflection and simple reflections","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___4___-Roots/Definition-6___4___10___-Reflection-and-simple-reflections/#Chapter6___Definition6___4___10"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___6___-Reflection-functors/Definition-6___6___3___-Reflection-functor-F_i______/#Chapter6___Definition6___6___3":{"contents":"Definition 6.6.3. Let Q be a quiver, and let i \\in Q be a sink. Let V be a representation of Q. Then we define the reflection functor\n\nF_i^+ : \\operatorname{Rep} Q \\to \\operatorname{Rep} \\overline{Q}_i\n\nby the rule\n\nF_i^+(V)_k = V_k \\quad \\text{if } k \\neq i,\n\nF_i^+(V)_i = \\ker\\left(\\varphi : \\bigoplus_{j \\to i} V_j \\to V_i\\right).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.6. Reflection functors\u0009Definition 6.6.3: Reflection functor F_i^+","header":"Definition 6.6.3: Reflection functor F_i^+","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___6___-Reflection-functors/Definition-6___6___3___-Reflection-functor-F_i______/#Chapter6___Definition6___6___3"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___7___-Morita-equivalence/Basic-algebra/#Chapter9___Definition9___7___2":{"contents":"Definition 9.7.2. A finite dimensional algebra B with commutative B/\\operatorname{Rad}(B) is called basic.\n\nSo we have the following corollary of Theorem 9.6.4.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.7. Morita equivalence\u0009Basic algebra","header":"Basic algebra","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___7___-Morita-equivalence/Basic-algebra/#Chapter9___Definition9___7___2"},"/Frontmatter/Blank-Page-1-___-Blank-page/Blank-Page-1-___-Blank-page/#Frontmatter___BlankPage1":{"contents":"[This page is intentionally left blank.]\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u0009Frontmatter\u0009Blank Page 1 — Blank page","header":"Blank Page 1 — Blank page","id":"/Frontmatter/Blank-Page-1-___-Blank-page/Blank-Page-1-___-Blank-page/#Frontmatter___BlankPage1"}});